{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond inherited the county at a young age and faced immediate challenges due to his minority. His early rule was marked by regency and internal instability as powerful nobles vied for control. He was eventually crowned in his own right but struggled to assert authority over the fractious nobility of the county. His reign coincided with a turbulent period in the Crusader states as Muslim forces under Nur ad-Din and later Saladin began to consolidate power.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III was known for his cautious diplomacy and political maneuvering. He often sought alliances with Muslim rulers to counterbalance threats from rival Christian factions. His relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem was complex. He was briefly regent of Jerusalem after the death of Baldwin IV in 1185. During this time he tried to maintain peace with Saladin but faced opposition from the militant faction led by Guy of Lusignan. Raymond was also a key figure in the internal power struggles of the Crusader states. He was accused of treason by some nobles who believed he was too willing to negotiate with Muslim leaders. These accusations were fueled by his decision to make a truce with Saladin in 1180 which many saw as a betrayal of Christian interests.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly after the disastrous Battle of Hattin where the Crusader army was annihilated by Saladin. He had been wounded in the battle and did not survive the aftermath. His death marked the end of an era of cautious leadership in the County of Tripoli. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV who inherited a weakened state on the brink of collapse. Raymond III's legacy is mixed. He was criticized for his indecisiveness and perceived lack of zeal in defending Christian territories. Yet he was also praised for his attempts to preserve peace in a time of relentless warfare. His efforts to stabilize Tripoli and manage complex alliances reflect the difficult realities of ruling a Crusader state surrounded by powerful enemies. His life illustrates the intricate balance between diplomacy and military action in the Levant during the twelfth century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -5.1973900554003194e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0013335392577573657}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.242440288886428e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.018150780349969864}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.536697689327411e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.0046993764117360115}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.006416789256036282}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.08058454096317291}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -8.583032467868179e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009176096064038575}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0002522150462027639}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -4.51792984677013e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.38688698410987854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0028567002154886723}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.5735502529423684e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8